// MULTIPLICATIVE BINDS TIGHTER THAN ADDITIVE — the one tier line every
// other tier is calibrated against.
//
// `2 + 3 * 4` is 14, not 20. The interesting direction is not the pretty
// case but the SIGNED one: `10 - 6 / 2` is 7, and a compiler that folded
// division into the additive tier produces 2 — a plausible-looking small
// number, not a crash. `%` and `/` sit in the SAME row as `*` (D1 calls
// `+%`/`*%` the wrapping forms of the same operations, so there is exactly
// one multiplicative tier, never one row per operator):
//
//   `11 % 4 * 10` is 30 read left-to-right inside the tier; `%` dropped
//   into the additive tier gives 11. `2 * 11 % 4` is 2; `%` raised above
//   `*` gives 6. Both wrong answers are ordinary numbers.
//
// `20 / 3 * 3` is 18 — the truncating division that makes the grouping
// visible at all; an exact-divisor spelling like `40 / 4 * 2` is 20 under
// either reading and proves nothing.
//
// THE ONE-LINE BREAK: in the parser's binary-operator table (or its
// hand-written equivalent), move `* / % *%` onto the additive
// precedence — or equivalently delete the multiplicative tier. Any single
// line below then prints a different number, and none of them rejects.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    println("mul {}", 2 + 3 * 4);
    println("div {}", 10 - 6 / 2);
    println("modmul {}", 11 % 4 * 10);
    println("mulmod {}", 2 * 11 % 4);
    println("divtrunc {}", 20 / 3 * 3);

    // all four operators in one flat expression: 2 + 12 - 2. The
    // fully-flattened left fold gives (((2+3)*4-6)/3) = 4.
    println("sink {}", 2 + 3 * 4 - 6 / 3);
    Ok(0);
}
